{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae1b940ba0683beb", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tLeveraging Gradient Reversal Loss and Multitask Learning for\n2\tDatasets-Aware Audio Deepfake Detection\n3\tMingrui Liang1 , Thomas Thebaud1 , Łukasz Wójciak2 , Laureano Moro Velazquez1 , Yishay Carmiel2 ,\n4\tJesus Villalba Lopez1 , Najim Dehak1,2\n5\t1\n6\t\n7\tDepartment of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Johns Hopkins University, USA\n8\t2\n9\tMeaning, USA\n10\tmliang17@jh.edu, tthebau1@jhu.edu,\n11\t\n12\tarXiv:2607.23961v1 [eess.AS] 27 Jul 2026\n13\t\n14\tAbstract\n15\tRecent advances in speech synthesis and voice conversion,\n16\twhich pose threats to security and privacy, have underscored\n17\tthe need for deepfake detection technology. Although existing detection systems achieve strong performance on individual\n18\tdatasets, they often fail to generalize across diverse datasets.\n19\tPrior methods for improving generalization, including data augmentation, adversarial training on auxiliary factors such as language or codec types, and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE), are limited by predefined augmentation coverage, difficulties in obtaining auxiliary factors, and substantial model complexity. In this\n20\twork, we propose a practical dataset-aware framework for deepfake detection. Our method targets heterogeneous datasets for\n21\twhich auxiliary annotations such as language, codec, or spoofing method may not be consistently available. We therefore\n22\trely only on dataset identity as a naturally available supervisory\n23\tsignal for multitask (MT) and gradient reversal layer (GRL)\n24\ttraining, allowing the model to investigate both dataset-aware\n25\tmultitask supervision and adversarial suppression of datasetspecific information. We conduct experiments following the\n26\t2025 Speech DeepFake Arena benchmark protocol, evaluating our model across multiple evaluation datasets and reporting aggregate performance in terms of Equal Error Rate (EER),\n27\tincluding Average EER and Pooled EER. Compared with the\n28\tbaseline, MT reduces Average EER by 13.14% relatively, while\n29\tGRL reduces Pooled EER by 5.32% relatively. These results\n30\tdemonstrate that our method can improve aggregate detection\n31\tperformance across heterogeneous evaluation datasets, offering\n32\ta practical solution for deploying reliable deepfake detection\n33\tsystems on diverse and unseen real-world data.\n34\tIndex Terms: DeepFake detection, speech anti-spoofing, SSL,\n35\tmultitask, GRL\n36\tProject links:\n37\tMT model repository 1 and GRL model repository2 .\n38\t\n39\t1. Introduction\n40\tWith the help of powerful deep neural networks, recent advances in text-to-speech (TTS) and voice conversion (VC)\n41\thave significantly improved the perceptual quality of generated\n42\tspeech. Although this technique is widely used across domains,\n43\tsuch as data enhancement [1], it can also be used for criminal\n44\tactivities, including financial fraud, political conflict, and impersonation. Therefore, audio deepfake detection has gradually\n45\t1 https://huggingface.co/RuiRuihigh/\n46\thyperion-mt-deepfake-detector\n47\t2 https://huggingface.co/RuiRuihigh/\n48\thyperion-grl-deepfake-detector\n49\t\n50\tFigure 1: Overview of the unified data construction. After aggregating datasets from diverse sources, only bona fide/spoof\n51\tlabels and dataset identity remain consistently available across\n52\tdatasets. The Curated, Challenge, and In-the-Wild categories\n53\tindicate the primary grouping of each dataset and are not intended to be strictly mutually exclusive.\n54\t\n55\tbecome an active research field [2, 3]. Initially, researchers primarily used handcrafted features, such as CQT, STFT, and LF,\n56\tas inputs to classify audio [4], achieving excellent results. Later,\n57\tas the self-supervised learning (SSL) models become more and\n58\tmore powerful, people begin to take advantage of them in different tasks in the speech area, such as automatic speech recognition (ASR) [5], speaker verification (SV) [6], and surely audio\n59\tdeepfake detection (ADD) [7]. By leveraging large-scale pretraining corpora and learning rich representations, SSL models significantly enhance the robustness and generalization of\n60\tanti-spoofing systems [8–10]. Currently, state-of-the-art systems typically combine SSL models with advanced neural backbones [7].\n61\tAlthough these systems have achieved remarkable performance in controlled scenarios [11, 12], the challenge of effectively generalizing their capabilities to unseen data and increasingly sophisticated speech deepfakes remains critical. This issue is further complicated by the unique characteristics of the\n62\tstate-of-the-art speech deepfake datasets, including diverse generation methods, multilingual data, and varied post-processing,\n63\twhich make them reasonable to consider as distinct domains.\n64\tCurrently, the most common approach to address the domain gap is data augmentation, in which variable masks or\n65\tcodec types are applied to audio. Another approach is adversarial training with labels such as language or codec types,\n66\twhich can help the model learn language-invariant or codec-\n67\t\n68\t\finvariant information [13–15]. Furthermore, MoE provides a\n69\tnatural way to handle domain shift by routing different experts\n70\tto process data from their respective domains, thereby enabling\n71\tdomain-specific specialization. However, these approaches remain difficult to scale to heterogeneous training data. Data\n72\taugmentation is restricted to predefined transformations and\n73\tmay not cover real-world dataset shifts. Adversarial training\n74\tbased on language or codec labels requires auxiliary annotations that are often unavailable or inconsistently defined across\n75\tdatasets, while MoE-based methods introduce additional model\n76\tcomplexity and computational cost. This annotation issue becomes more evident when datasets from different sources are\n77\tcombined, as illustrated in Fig. 1. In such a unified collection,\n78\tmetadata such as language, spoofing method, and codec type\n79\tcannot be consistently obtained for all samples. In contrast,\n80\tbona fide/spoof labels and dataset identity are reliably available\n81\tacross datasets, making dataset identity a practical supervisory\n82\tsignal for mixed-dataset training.\n83\tMotivated by this observation, we propose a dataset-aware\n84\tframework for training with heterogeneous and unlabeled inthe-wild datasets. At the same time, our framework leverages\n85\tthe diversity of pooled datasets without relying on introducing substantial model complexity. Specifically, in the multitask (MT) learning, we introduce a complex auxiliary label by\n86\tcombining dataset identity with the spoof/bona fide label, forming class-conditional dataset labels such as dataset-spoof and\n87\tdataset-bona fide. This design ensures that the auxiliary task remains aligned with the main spoofing detection objective while\n88\tstill capturing dataset-specific characteristics. For the gradient\n89\treversal layer (GRL) training, we apply dataset identity directly\n90\tto encourage the feature extractor to reduce dataset-specific\n91\tbias. The key contributions of this work can be summarized\n92\tas follows:\n93\t• Adaptation to in-the-wild datasets: Our framework uses\n94\tdataset identity as a naturally available supervisory signal,\n95\tavoiding the need for auxiliary annotations, which are often\n96\tunavailable or inconsistent across datasets. This makes newly\n97\tcollected or unlabeled in-the-wild datasets easier to integrate\n98\tinto mixed-dataset training.\n99\t• Dataset-aware supervision: We introduce class-conditional\n100\tdataset labels by combining dataset identity with bona\n101\tfide/spoof labels. Compared with using dataset identity\n102\talone, this supervision better aligns the auxiliary task with\n103\tthe main spoofing detection objective and helps the model\n104\tcapture dataset-specific variations.\n105\t• Improved detection with a lightweight single-model\n106\tframework: Our approach achieves a 13.14% relative improvement in Average EER and a 5.32% relative improvement in Pooled EER, demonstrating stronger cross-dataset\n107\tgeneralization. Unlike MoE- or ensemble-based methods,\n108\tour framework trains a single detector with 315.4M parameters, reducing training and inference complexity while maintaining strong performance.\n109\t\n110\t2. Related Work\n111\t2.1. Hand-crafted Feature-based Audio Deepfake Detection\n112\tHand-crafted features (e.g., Mel Spectrogram) are widely used\n113\tacross many speech-related fields. In preliminary audio deepfake detection work, these features indeed achieved some remarkable results [16, 17]. For example, HM-Conformer [18]\n114\t\n115\tintroduced the hierarchical pooling and multi-level classification token aggregation into Conformer [19], transferring LFCC\n116\tto two-class prediction. Additionally, some work used a combination of a special log-Mel spectrogram and ResNet [20] to\n117\taddress deepfake detection tasks, achieving robust results.\n118\t2.2. Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) Model-based Audio\n119\tDeepfake Detection\n120\tSSL models are usually pretrained on large amounts of unlabeled speech data with self-supervised learning techniques.\n121\tAmong these SSL models, the most popular models are WavLM\n122\t[21], HuBERT [22], Wav2Vec2 2.0 [23], and XLS-R [24].\n123\tCompared with hand-crafted features, SSL models can capture\n124\tricher and more meaningful speech representations that can be\n125\teffectively used in downstream tasks.\n126\tBeyond simply obtaining embeddings from the last layers\n127\tof SSL, recent work leverages information from multiple layers\n128\tin SSL models using various techniques [25–28]. For instance,\n129\tZhang et al. [26] leverage Sensitive Layer Select (SLS) to fuse\n130\trepresentative layers automatically, and Guo et al. [27] propose\n131\tusing attentive statistic pooling on every layer in WavLM to\n132\timprove accuracy.\n133\tWith SSL models and advanced backbone models (e.g.,\n134\tECAPA-TDNN [29], BiCrossMamba-ST [30], and AASIST\n135\t[31]), researchers can achieve great results. Given that XLS-R\n136\ttypically achieves more competitive performance [32] and that\n137\tspeaker information can contribute to classification [17], we use\n138\tthe combination of XLS-R and ECAPA-TDNN as our baseline\n139\tmodel.\n140\t2.3. Domain Generalization and Robustness\n141\tAlthough current models have thousands of parameters and are\n142\thighly effective at discriminating real audio from fake audio,\n143\tthey still fall short in generalization [4]. When these models\n144\tare exposed to unseen conditions, such as novel spoofing algorithms, cross-dataset variations, and different recording channels, their performance will decline markedly.\n145\tOne way to improve robustness is to introduce data variability by data augmentation [33–35]. For instance, RawBoost [35]\n146\tenhances robustness by applying a series of realistic signal-level\n147\tperturbations directly to raw waveforms, including channel effects, additive noise, and nonlinear distortions.\n148\tAnother direction focuses on learning domain-invariant\n149\trepresentations through multitasking and adversarial domain\n150\tadaptation. Specifically, multitasking explicitly retains domainspecific information, whereas adversarial domain adaptation\n151\taims to align feature distributions across domains by removing domain-specific factors. Previous work has primarily leveraged auxiliary classifiers and gradient reversal on auxiliary labels such as language, device, and codec types [13–15].\n152\tMore recently, model specialization strategies have been\n153\texplored to improve generalization. Negroni et al. propose a\n154\tMixture-of-Experts (MoE) framework, where multiple domainspecific detectors are combined through a gating mechanism\n155\tto dynamically adapt to different input conditions [36]. This\n156\tapproach leverages domain-specific knowledge and improves\n157\tadaptability to diverse datasets.\n158\tDespite their effectiveness, these approaches remain limited in real-world scenarios. Data augmentation improves robustness by increasing data diversity, but it does not fundamentally address unseen distributions, as deepfake techniques\n159\tcontinue to evolve [7]. Existing MT and adversarial adaptation methods often use auxiliary metadata, such as language,\n160\t\n161\t\fFigure 2: Overview of the proposed framework. The blue modules represent the baseline detection pipeline. MT consists of the blue\n162\tmodules and the green auxiliary branch for multitask learning, while GRL consists of the blue modules and the orange auxiliary branch.\n163\tThe snowflake and flame icons denote frozen and trainable modules, respectively, with the feature extractor frozen in Stage 1 and all\n164\tmodules trainable in Stage 2.\n165\t\n166\tcodec, or synthesis method, as supervision. However, these labels are frequently unavailable or inconsistently defined across\n167\tdatasets, limiting their applicability in large-scale mixed-dataset\n168\ttraining. Meanwhile, model specialization approaches such as\n169\tMixture-of-Experts require training multiple large models and\n170\tadditional gating mechanisms, leading to high computational\n171\tcost and poor scalability [37]. These limitations highlight the\n172\tneed for more flexible and efficient approaches for domain generalization.\n173\t\n174\t3. Method\n175\tTo make the system robust across scenarios, the key is to enable\n176\tit to learn characteristics that are irrelevant to those scenarios,\n177\tsuch as dataset-specific ones. Considering this, we propose two\n178\tmethods to explicitly allow the system to understand the special\n179\tinformation: multitask (MT) and gradient reversal layer (GRL).\n180\tOur baseline builds on the SSL-based spoofing detection\n181\tframework explored in [54], where SSL front-end representations combined with an ECAPA-TDNN backbone are shown to\n182\tbe effective for generalizing to unseen audio data. As shown\n183\tin the blue modules of Fig. 2, raw audio waveforms are first\n184\tprocessed by an XLS-R encoder to extract high-level representations. Beyond simply using the encoder’s last layer, we employ attentive fusion to integrate outputs from multiple encoder\n185\tlayers. The fused feature is then fed into a convolutional backbone network, where we modify the ECAPA-TDNN from three\n186\tSE-Res2Blocks to a single SE-Res2Block to capture both temporal and frequency-related dependencies. An attentive statistical pooling layer is applied to aggregate variable frame-level\n187\tfeatures into a fixed embedding. Finally, a fully connected classifier head is used to predict the binary label, spoof or bona fide.\n188\tTotally, the whole framework only contains 315.4M parameters.\n189\t\n190\t3.1. Multitask Learning\n191\tIn MT (the blue and green modules) of Fig. 2, we adopt a multitask learning strategy to explicitly model dataset-dependent\n192\tvariations. For each input sample x, we denote its spoofing\n193\tlabel as y ∈ {bona fide, spoof} and its dataset identity as d. Instead of using the dataset identity alone as the auxiliary label,\n194\twe construct a class-conditional dataset label:\n195\tMT\n196\tyaux\n197\t= (d, y),\n198\t\n199\t(1)\n200\t\n201\twhich corresponds to labels such as dataset-bona fide and\n202\tdataset-spoof.\n203\tGiven an input sample x, the backbone extracts a feature\n204\trepresentation:\n205\tf = F(x).\n206\t(2)\n207\tThe main classifier predicts the binary spoofing label:\n208\tzmain = Cmain (f ),\n209\t\n210\t(3)\n211\t\n212\twhile the auxiliary classifier predicts the class-conditional\n213\tdataset label:\n214\tMT\n215\tMT\n216\tzaux\n217\t= Caux\n218\t(f ).\n219\t(4)\n220\tThe corresponding losses are:\n221\tLmain = CE(zmain , y),\n222\t\n223\t(5)\n224\t\n225\tMT\n226\tMT\n227\tLMT\n228\taux = CE(zaux , yaux ),\n229\t\n230\t(6)\n231\t\n232\twhere CE(·) denotes the cross-entropy loss. The overall multitask objective is:\n233\tLMT = Lmain + λMT LMT\n234\taux ,\n235\twhere λMT controls the contribution of the auxiliary task.\n236\t\n237\t(7)\n238\t\n239\t\fTable 1: Summary of the datasets used for training, validation, and evaluation, including language coverage and the total number of\n240\tutterances.\n241\tDataset\n242\t\n243\tLanguage\n244\t\n245\tTotal #utt\n246\t\n247\tTrain\n248\t\n249\tDev\n250\t\n251\tASVspoof 2015 [38]\n252\tASVspoof 2019 [39]\n253\tASVspoof 2024 [40]\n254\tFake or Real [41]\n255\tDFADD [42]\n256\tMLAAD [43]\n257\tCodecfake [44]\n258\tSpoofCeleb [45]\n259\tLibriSpeech [46]\n260\tVoicePrivacy 2024 [47]\n261\tSONAR [48]\n262\tLibriSeVoc [49]\n263\tASVspoof 2021 [50]\n264\tIn The Wild [51]\n265\tADD 2022 [52]\n266\tADD 2023 [53]\n267\t\n268\tEnglish\n269\tEnglish\n270\tEnglish\n271\tEnglish\n272\tEnglish\n273\tMultilingual\n274\tEnglish\n275\tEnglish\n276\tEnglish\n277\tEnglish\n278\tMultilingual\n279\tEnglish\n280\tEnglish\n281\tEnglish\n282\tChinese\n283\tChinese\n284\t\n285\t263,151\n286\t121,461\n287\t1,004,078\n288\t37,541\n289\t191,039\n290\t54,000\n291\t1,172,314\n292\t2,631,551\n293\t281,241\n294\t728,098\n295\t3,947\n296\t92,407\n297\t181,566\n298\t31,779\n299\t404,329\n300\t285,861\n301\t\n302\t✓\n303\t✓\n304\t✓\n305\t✓\n306\t✓\n307\t✓\n308\t✓\n309\t✓\n310\t✓\n311\t✓\n312\t✓\n313\t✓\n314\t\n315\t✓\n316\t✓\n317\t✓\n318\t✓\n319\t✓\n320\t✓\n321\t✓\n322\t✓\n323\t✓\n324\t✓\n325\t✓\n326\t✓\n327\t\n328\tEval\n329\t✓\n330\t✓\n331\t✓\n332\t✓\n333\t✓\n334\t\n335\t✓\n336\t✓\n337\t✓\n338\t✓\n339\t✓\n340\t✓\n341\t\n342\tNote: Dev denotes the validation split, and Total #utt denotes the sum of all available utterances for each dataset. For ADD 2022 and ADD\n343\t2023, all provided tasks and evaluation rounds are included.\n344\t\n345\tThis class-conditional auxiliary supervision encourages the\n346\trepresentation to capture dataset-specific variations while remaining aligned with the main spoofing detection objective.\n347\tCompared with predicting dataset identity alone, our proposed\n348\tlabel reduces ambiguity because the auxiliary task is conditioned on the bona fide/spoof distinction rather than being independent of it.\n349\t3.2. Gradient Reversal Layer\n350\tIn GRL (the blue and orange modules) of Fig. 2, we adopt a\n351\tgradient reversal layer to learn dataset-invariant representations.\n352\tUnlike multitask learning, which encourages the backbone to\n353\tencode dataset-related information, GRL discourages the backbone from preserving information that allows the dataset identity to be predicted.\n354\tGiven the feature representation:\n355\tf = F(x),\n356\t\n357\t(8)\n358\t\n359\twe pass it through a GRL before feeding it into the auxiliary\n360\tdataset classifier:\n361\tf˜ = GRL(f ),\n362\t(9)\n363\tGRL\n364\tGRL ˜\n365\t(f ).\n366\t(10)\n367\tzaux = Caux\n368\tFor GRL training, the auxiliary label is the dataset identity:\n369\tGRL\n370\tyaux\n371\t= d.\n372\t\n373\t(11)\n374\t\n375\tThe auxiliary loss is therefore:\n376\tGRL\n377\tLGRL\n378\taux = CE(zaux , d).\n379\t\n380\t(12)\n381\t\n382\tDuring the forward pass, the GRL acts as an identity function:\n383\tf˜ = GRL(f ) = f.\n384\t\n385\t(13)\n386\t\n387\tDuring backpropagation, however, it reverses the gradient\n388\tpassed to the backbone:\n389\t∂LGRL\n390\t∂LGRL\n391\taux\n392\t= − aux .\n393\t∂f\n394\t∂ f˜\n395\t\n396\t(14)\n397\t\n398\tThe training loss is written as:\n399\tLGRL = Lmain + λGRL LGRL\n400\taux ,\n401\t\n402\t(15)\n403\t\n404\twhere λGRL controls the strength of the auxiliary objective. The\n405\tadversarial effect is introduced by the GRL during backpropagation, rather than by explicitly subtracting the auxiliary loss\n406\tfrom the training objective.\n407\tSpecifically, the auxiliary classifier is optimized normally\n408\tto predict the dataset identity. However, for the backbone, the\n409\teffective gradient becomes:\n410\t∂LGRL\n411\t∂Lmain\n412\t∂LGRL\n413\t=\n414\t− λGRL aux ,\n415\t∂θF\n416\t∂θF\n417\t∂θF\n418\t\n419\t(16)\n420\t\n421\twhere θF denotes the parameters of the backbone.\n422\tTherefore, the auxiliary classifier learns to identify the\n423\tdataset source, while the backbone learns to make this prediction difficult. This encourages the learned representation to\n424\tremove dataset-discriminative information, thereby promoting\n425\tdataset-invariant features and improving cross-dataset generalization.\n426\t\n427\t4. Experiment\n428\t4.1. Datasets\n429\tWe construct a large-scale unified corpus for training and validation by combining multiple publicly available datasets. The\n430\tusage of each dataset across the training, validation, and evaluation splits is summarized in Table 1. Specifically, for training and validation, we have ASVspoof 2015, ASVspoof 2019,\n431\tASVspoof 2024, Fake or Real, DFADD, MLAAD, Codecfake,\n432\tSpoofceleb, Librispeech, VoicePrivacy 2024, SONAR and LibriSeVoc. These corpora cover diverse spoofing techniques,\n433\trecording conditions, speaker characteristics, and languages,\n434\tproviding broad supervision for robust speech deepfake detection.\n435\tThe resulting training subset contains 7,325.65 hours of audio and 4,859,430 utterances in total. Among them, 4,198,675\n436\t\n437\t\fare spoof samples and 660,755 are bona fide samples, accounting for 86.4% and 13.6% of the training utterances, respectively.\n438\tFor evaluation, we follow the benchmark protocol of\n439\tthe 2025 Speech Deepfake Arena [55], where the evaluation\n440\tdatasets include In The Wild, ASVspoof 2019, ASVspoof 2021,\n441\tASVspoof 2024, Fake or Real, Codecfake, ADD 2022, ADD\n442\t2023, DFADD, LibriSeVoc, and SONAR. The evaluation suite\n443\tconsists of multiple in-domain and out-of-domain test sets, enabling a comprehensive assessment of model generalization\n444\tacross heterogeneous speech deepfake scenarios.\n445\t4.2. Auxiliary Labels\n446\tIn this work, the design of auxiliary labels plays a crucial role\n447\tin guiding the learning behavior of different components in our\n448\tframework. For multitask learning, we construct auxiliary labels by combining dataset identity with spoof/bona fide annotations, forming joint labels of the form dataset × spoof/bona\n449\tfide. As a result, the model is encouraged to learn distinctions\n450\tacross datasets and spoofing conditions, thereby enhancing its\n451\tdiscriminative capability in multi-domain scenarios. In contrast, for the GRL-based method, we employ only the dataset\n452\tidentity as the auxiliary label. The motivation is that GRL aims\n453\tto learn domain-invariant representations. If spoof/bona fide information is also included in the auxiliary labels, the adversarial\n454\ttraining may remove useful cues for spoof detection. Therefore,\n455\twe use dataset-only labels so that GRL focuses on eliminating\n456\tdomain differences while preserving spoof-related information.\n457\t4.3. Training Setup\n458\tWe adopt a two-stage training strategy to stabilize optimization\n459\tand improve representation learning.\n460\tData processing. For both stages, we apply data augmentation including reverberation and noise perturbation, with up to\n461\tsix augmented variants per sample. Training samples are generated using a class-weighted random segment sampler with a\n462\tfixed chunk length of 2 seconds, ensuring balanced sampling\n463\tacross different datasets. For validation, we use a bucketing\n464\tsampler to handle variable-length inputs efficiently.\n465\tStage 1. In the first stage, we freeze the SSL feature extractor and train only the fuser, downstream backbone, and classifiers, which allows the model to learn stable representations\n466\twithout disrupting the pretrained features. During this stage,\n467\twe use stochastic gradient descent (SGD) with a relatively large\n468\tlearning rate (0.4) and exponential decay scheduling. The effective batch size is set to 1024, and the model is trained with\n469\tmixed-precision training.\n470\tStage 2. In the second stage, we unfreeze the entire model\n471\tand perform full training. The learning rate is reduced to\n472\t5 × 10−3 with a similar exponential decay schedule to ensure\n473\tstable optimization. The effective batch size is set to 512, and\n474\twe adopt the additive angular margin softmax (AAM-Softmax)\n475\tloss with scale s = 32 and margin m = 0.2 for the main classification task.\n476\tIn both stages, the auxiliary classifier or adapter is trainable,\n477\tand we employ early stopping to prevent overfitting. Additionally, for both MT and GRL, we set the auxiliary loss weight λ to\n478\t0.1 based on preliminary experiments on a smaller development\n479\tsubset.\n480\t\n481\tTable 2: Evaluation results on the 2025 Speech Deepfake Arena\n482\tbenchmark. Results are reported in terms of EER (%), where\n483\tlower is better. The MT system uses Dataset Type × Spoof Detection labels as auxiliary supervision, while the GRL system\n484\tuses Dataset Type labels for adversarial training.\n485\tDataset\n486\t\n487\tBaseline\n488\t\n489\tMT\n490\t\n491\tGRL\n492\t\n493\tIn the Wild\n494\tASV2019\n495\tASV2021LA\n496\tASV2021DF\n497\tASV2024-EVAL\n498\tFake or Real\n499\tCodecfake\n500\tADD 2022 Track 1\n501\tADD 2022 Track 3\n502\tADD 2023 R1\n503\tADD 2023 R2\n504\tDFADD\n505\tLibriSeVoc\n506\tSONAR\n507\t\n508\t5.211\n509\t5.850\n510\t10.940\n511\t5.261\n512\t13.263\n513\t1.428\n514\t19.990\n515\t23.951\n516\t5.275\n517\t15.978\n518\t23.935\n519\t0.747\n520\t0.056\n521\t0.885\n522\t\n523\t4.778\n524\t1.526\n525\t6.986\n526\t4.041\n527\t14.371\n528\t0.057\n529\t15.084\n530\t21.912\n531\t4.535\n532\t19.967\n533\t21.233\n534\t0.095\n535\t0.116\n536\t0.632\n537\t\n538\t3.062\n539\t4.453\n540\t13.712\n541\t5.043\n542\t13.538\n543\t4.583\n544\t18.980\n545\t22.570\n546\t5.718\n547\t18.389\n548\t18.290\n549\t0.214\n550\t0.662\n551\t2.097\n552\t\n553\tAverage EER\n554\tPooled EER\n555\t\n556\t9.484\n557\t12.596\n558\t\n559\t8.238 9.379\n560\t13.050 11.926\n561\t\n562\tNote: ASV2019 denotes ASVspoof 2019; ASV2021LA and\n563\tASV2021DF denote the LA and DF tracks of ASVspoof 2021,\n564\trespectively; ASV2024-EVAL denotes the evaluation set of ASVspoof\n565\t2024.\n566\t\n567\t5. Results and Analysis\n568\t5.1. MT & GRL Results\n569\tTable 2 presents the evaluation results of the baseline, multitask learning (MT), and gradient reversal layer (GRL) methods on the 2025 Speech Deepfake Arena benchmark. Overall,\n570\tMT achieves the best Average EER, reducing the baseline from\n571\t9.484% to 8.238%, corresponding to a relative improvement\n572\tof 13.14%. This indicates that explicitly introducing auxiliary\n573\tdomain-related supervision improves the average detection performance across diverse evaluation datasets.\n574\tAt the dataset level, MT obtains the best performance on\n575\t9 out of 14 evaluation subsets, including ASVspoof 2019,\n576\tASVspoof 2021LA, ASVspoof 2021DF, Fake or Real, Codecfake, ADD 2022 Track 1, ADD 2022 Track 3, DFADD, and\n577\tSONAR. In particular, MT leads to substantial improvements\n578\ton several challenging subsets, such as ASVspoof 2019, where\n579\tthe EER decreases from 5.850% to 1.526%, and Fake or Real,\n580\twhere the EER decreases from 1.428% to 0.057%. These\n581\tresults suggest that the MT objective can effectively exploit\n582\tdataset-level supervision to improve generalization across diverse evaluation datasets.\n583\tCompared with MT, GRL shows a different behavior. Although GRL does not achieve the best Average EER, it obtains\n584\tthe best Pooled EER, reducing the baseline from 12.596% to\n585\t11.926%, corresponding to a relative improvement of 5.32%.\n586\tGRL also achieves the best result on In the Wild and ADD 2023\n587\tR2. This suggests that GRL may be more beneficial when aggregating trials across datasets under a shared decision threshold, potentially due to improved cross-dataset score consistency.\n588\tTable 3 further compares our systems with top-performing\n589\topen systems on the 2025 Speech Deepfake Arena leaderboard.\n590\t\n591\t\f(a) Baseline\n592\t\n593\t(b) MT\n594\t\n595\t(c) GRL\n596\t\n597\tFigure 3: t-SNE visualization of learned embeddings under different training strategies. Each row corresponds to one model configuration: Baseline, MT, and GRL. Within each row, the two panels show the same embedding space colored by Dataset type labels and\n598\tSpoof det labels, respectively.\n599\t“‘\n600\tAmong the open systems summarized in the table, MT achieves\n601\tthe third-best Average EER, while GRL achieves the third-best\n602\tPooled EER. Notably, the two systems that outperform ours\n603\tuse substantially larger models, with 500M and 1000M parameters, whereas our MT and GRL systems use 315.4M parameters.\n604\tThis comparison suggests that the proposed training strategies\n605\tprovide a favorable trade-off between detection performance\n606\tand model size, achieving competitive leaderboard performance\n607\twithout relying on substantially larger model capacity.\n608\t\n609\tThese observations reveal distinct performance profiles for\n610\tMT and GRL. MT improves the average performance across\n611\tindividual datasets through explicit, dataset-aware auxiliary supervision, whereas GRL yields better pooled performance under an objective designed to reduce dataset-specific information. Therefore, MT is more effective in improving the macroaverage of per-dataset EERs, whereas GRL can be advantageous for pooled evaluation across heterogeneous datasets.\n612\t\n613\t\fTable 3: Comparison with top-performing open systems on the\n614\t2025 Speech Deepfake Arena benchmark. Params denotes the\n615\tnumber of parameters in millions. Results are reported as EER\n616\t(%), where lower is better. The best, second-best, and thirdbest results are indicated by bold, italics, and underline, respectively.\n617\tSystem\n618\t\n619\tParams (M)\n620\t\n621\tAvg. Pooled\n622\t\n623\tDF Arena 1B V 1 [56]\n624\tDF Arena 500M V 1 [56]\n625\tXLSR+SLS [26]\n626\tTCM [57]\n627\tBiCrossMamba-ST [30]\n628\tNes2NetX [58]\n629\tWav2Vec2 AASIST [10]\n630\tXLSR Mamba [59]\n631\tWhisper Mesonet [60]\n632\t\n633\t1000 5.919\n634\t500 5.780\n635\t340 14.015\n636\t319 15.846\n637\t318.2 15.778\n638\t317.9 16.186\n639\t317.8 18.138\n640\t319 14.647\n641\t7.6 28.355\n642\t\n643\tMT (Ours)\n644\tGRL (Ours)\n645\t\n646\t315.4\n647\t315.4\n648\t\n649\t9.524\n650\t10.880\n651\t16.079\n652\t16.691\n653\t17.154\n654\t17.366\n655\t19.607\n656\t20.591\n657\t23.551\n658\t\n659\t8.238 13.050\n660\t9.379 11.926\n661\t\n662\t5.2. Visualization Analysis\n663\tFig. 3 shows the t-SNE visualization of the learned embeddings\n664\textracted after the backbone pooling layer. For each training\n665\tstrategy, the two panels in the same row correspond to the same\n666\tembedding space, but are colored according to Dataset type,\n667\twhich denotes the source dataset category, and Spoof det labels, which denotes the binary bona fide/spoof label, respectively. The visualized samples are drawn from the evaluation\n668\tsplit, with an equal number of bona fide and spoof samples sampled from each dataset. This visualization provides qualitative\n669\tevidence for how different training objectives affect the structure of the learned representation.\n670\tFor the baseline model, the embeddings exhibit noticeable\n671\tdataset-dependent clustering. Samples from the same dataset\n672\ttend to occupy similar local regions, indicating that the baseline representation contains strong dataset-specific information.\n673\tHowever, when colored by Spoof det labels, bona fide and\n674\tspoof samples are not clearly separated into two global clusters. This suggests that the baseline model may partially rely\n675\ton dataset-related biases rather than learning purely spoofdiscriminative cues.\n676\tCompared with the baseline, MT produces a more structured feature space with respect to Dataset type labels. Samples\n677\tfrom the same dataset form clearer local clusters, which is consistent with the design of the MT objective. Since MT explicitly\n678\tintroduces an auxiliary task to model dataset-related information, the backbone is encouraged to preserve domain-dependent\n679\tvariations. Meanwhile, the Spoof det visualization shows that\n680\tMT does not simply force all bona fide and spoof samples into\n681\ttwo global clusters. Instead, it appears to learn more finegrained spoof-discriminative structures within different dataset\n682\tregions. This observation is consistent with the quantitative results in Table 2, where MT achieves the best Average EER and\n683\tperforms best on most individual evaluation subsets.\n684\tIn contrast, GRL leads to a substantially more mixed embedding space across datasets. In the Dataset type visualization,\n685\tsamples from different datasets are more entangled, and the\n686\tdataset-specific clusters observed in the baseline and MT settings become less pronounced. This indicates that the adversarial objective imposed by GRL suppresses dataset-discriminative\n687\tinformation from the backbone representation. As a result,\n688\t\n689\tTable 4: Ablation study on auxiliary label design. Results are\n690\treported in terms of EER (%), where lower is better. The best\n691\tresult within each method group is shown in bold.\n692\tDataset\n693\t\n694\tMT\n695\t\n696\tGRL\n697\t\n698\tD\n699\t\n700\tD×S\n701\t\n702\tLang.\n703\t\n704\tD\n705\t\n706\tIn the Wild\n707\tASV2019\n708\tASV2021LA\n709\tASV2021DF\n710\tASV24-EVAL\n711\tFake or Real\n712\tCodecfake\n713\tADD22-T1\n714\tADD22-T3\n715\tADD 2023 R1\n716\tADD 2023 R2\n717\tDFADD\n718\tLibriSeVoc\n719\tSONAR\n720\t\n721\t3.072\n722\t7.518\n723\t16.037\n724\t5.706\n725\t13.506\n726\t1.165\n727\t17.655\n728\t28.283\n729\t5.312\n730\t16.101\n731\t26.695\n732\t0.000\n733\t0.038\n734\t0.862\n735\t\n736\t4.778\n737\t1.526\n738\t6.986\n739\t4.041\n740\t14.371\n741\t0.057\n742\t15.084\n743\t21.912\n744\t4.535\n745\t19.967\n746\t21.233\n747\t0.095\n748\t0.116\n749\t0.632\n750\t\n751\t3.276\n752\t9.353\n753\t15.697\n754\t4.644\n755\t11.512\n756\t2.289\n757\t24.398\n758\t24.347\n759\t6.176\n760\t20.745\n761\t29.856\n762\t1.305\n763\t0.049\n764\t3.034\n765\t\n766\t3.062\n767\t4.453\n768\t13.712\n769\t5.043\n770\t13.538\n771\t4.583\n772\t18.980\n773\t22.570\n774\t5.718\n775\t18.389\n776\t18.290\n777\t0.214\n778\t0.662\n779\t2.097\n780\t\n781\tAverage EER\n782\tPooled EER\n783\t\n784\t10.139\n785\t14.762\n786\t\n787\t8.238\n788\t13.050\n789\t\n790\t11.192\n791\t12.939\n792\t\n793\t9.379\n794\t11.926\n795\t\n796\tNote: D denotes Dataset, S denotes Spoof det, and Lang. denotes\n797\tLanguage. ASV2019: ASVspoof 2019; ASV2021LA & ASV2021DF:\n798\tthe LA and DF tracks of ASVspoof 2021, respectively; ASV24-EVAL:\n799\tthe evaluation set of ASVspoof 2024; ADD22-T1: ADD 2022 Track 1;\n800\tADD22-T3: ADD 2022 Track 3.\n801\t\n802\tGRL encourages a more domain-invariant feature space, where\n803\tsamples from heterogeneous datasets are better aligned. In the\n804\tSpoof det visualization, GRL also shows a more globally organized bona fide/spoof structure, suggesting that reducing dataset\n805\tseparability can help the model focus on spoof-related information shared across datasets. This is consistent with the Pooled\n806\tEER results in Table 2, where GRL achieves the best pooled\n807\tperformance.\n808\tOverall, the visualization reveals a clear difference between\n809\tthe two strategies. MT learns domain-aware representations\n810\tby explicitly modeling dataset-specific variations, which benefits per-dataset performance. GRL, on the other hand, learns\n811\tdomain-invariant representations by reducing dataset separability, which improves global generalization under heterogeneous\n812\tevaluation conditions. These qualitative findings support the\n813\tcomplementary behavior observed in the quantitative results.\n814\t5.3. Ablation Study Result\n815\tTable 4 presents the ablation study on different auxiliary label\n816\tdesigns for MT and GRL. For MT, we compare two auxiliary\n817\tsupervision strategies: dataset-only labels and Dataset type ×\n818\tSpoof det labels. For GRL, we compare language-based adversarial labels with dataset-based adversarial labels.\n819\tWhile prior MT-based studies typically use a single auxiliary label, such as language or codec type, our Dataset type\n820\t× Spoof det design jointly encodes dataset identity and bona\n821\tfide/spoof information. We therefore compare it with a datasetonly label design to verify the benefit of this combined auxiliary supervision. As shown in Table 4, using Dataset type ×\n822\tSpoof det labels consistently outperforms using dataset-only labels on most evaluation subsets. Specifically, Dataset type ×\n823\t\n824\t\fSpoof det achieves better results on 9 out of 14 datasets and\n825\treduces the Average EER from 10.139% to 8.238%. The improvement is especially clear on ASVspoof 2019, Fake or Real,\n826\tand ADD 2023 R2, where incorporating spoof/bona fide information into the auxiliary label provides substantially stronger\n827\tsupervision. These results indicate that simply modeling dataset\n828\tidentity is not sufficient for MT. Instead, combining dataset\n829\tidentity with spoof det labels allows the auxiliary task to capture both domain variation and spoof-related distribution differences, leading to more discriminative representations.\n830\tFor the GRL setting, we further investigate the choice of adversarial labels. Previous studies have also explored using language or codec-related attributes as auxiliary labels for adversarial training. However, these settings are usually conducted\n831\ton one or two datasets where such auxiliary annotations are naturally available. In contrast, our training setup mixes multiple\n832\tdatasets collected from different sources, making it difficult to\n833\tobtain consistent language or codec labels for all samples. In\n834\tparticular, codec-type annotations are not reliably available in\n835\tour collected training data. Therefore, we focus on comparing\n836\tdataset-based adversarial labels with language-based adversarial labels. To construct the language labels, we use a pre-trained\n837\tVoxLingua107 ECAPA-TDNN spoken language identification\n838\tmodel3 from SpeechBrain [61] to predict the language of each\n839\ttraining sample. This model achieves an error rate of 6.7% on\n840\tthe VoxLingua107 development set, providing reasonably reliable language predictions for constructing pseudo language labels at scale. The predicted language labels are then used as\n841\tpseudo labels for the GRL auxiliary classifier.\n842\tAs shown in Table 4, dataset-based adversarial training\n843\tachieves better aggregate performance than language-based adversarial training. GRL with dataset labels also performs better across most individual datasets. Compared with language\n844\tlabels, it reduces the Average EER from 11.192% to 9.379%\n845\tand the Pooled EER from 12.939% to 11.926%, corresponding to relative reductions of 16.20% and 7.83%, respectively.\n846\tThis suggests that dataset identity provides a more effective adversarial signal than language identity for encouraging domaininvariant feature learning in this benchmark. Since the evaluation datasets differ not only in language but also in recording\n847\tconditions, spoofing algorithms, data collection protocols, and\n848\tcorpus-specific artifacts, dataset labels can capture broader domain discrepancies than language labels alone.\n849\t\n850\t6. Conclusion\n851\tIn this work, we propose a dataset-aware framework to improve\n852\taudio deepfake detection across heterogeneous evaluation settings. Instead of relying on auxiliary annotations such as language, codec, or spoofing method, which are often unavailable\n853\tor inconsistent across datasets, our method uses dataset identity as a naturally available supervisory signal. Based on this\n854\tidea, we explore two distinct strategies: multitask learning with\n855\tDataset type × Spoof det labels and adversarial training with\n856\tdataset-based GRL.\n857\tExperimental results on the 2025 Speech Deepfake Arena\n858\tbenchmark show distinct performance gains for MT and GRL\n859\tunder Average EER and Pooled EER, respectively. MT\n860\tachieves the best Average EER, reducing it from 9.484% to\n861\t8.238%, while GRL achieves the best Pooled EER, reducing\n862\tit from 12.596% to 11.926%. The t-SNE visualization quali3 https://huggingface.co/speechbrain/\n863\tlang-id-voxlingua107-ecapa\n864\t\n865\ttatively suggests that MT encourages domain-aware representations, whereas GRL promotes more domain-invariant feature alignment. In addition, the ablation study confirms that\n866\tDataset type × Spoof det labels are more effective than datasetonly labels for MT, and dataset labels provide a stronger adversarial signal than language pseudo labels for GRL.\n867\tOverall, these results show that dataset identity can serve\n868\tas a simple and effective supervisory signal for improving aggregate detection performance across heterogeneous evaluation\n869\tdatasets.\n870\t\n871\t7. Limitation & Future Work\n872\tDespite the promising results, this work still has several limitations. First, MT and GRL are investigated as two separate\n873\tstrategies. As shown in the results, MT achieves better Average EER by learning domain-aware representations, while GRL\n874\tachieves better Pooled EER by encouraging domain-invariant\n875\trepresentations. However, we have not yet developed a unified\n876\tframework that can effectively combine these two complementary behaviors.\n877\tSecond, our experiments are conducted based on one backbone architecture. Although the proposed auxiliary label designs improve performance in this setting, it remains unclear\n878\twhether the same MT and GRL modules can consistently benefit other deepfake detection backbones, such as different SSL\n879\tencoders, pooling strategies, or classifier architectures.\n880\tFor future work, we plan to explore a unified training objective that jointly leverages the strengths of MT and GRL, allowing the model to adaptively balance domain-aware and domaininvariant learning. In addition, we will evaluate the proposed\n881\tdataset-aware training strategies on more backbone architectures to verify their general applicability and robustness across\n882\tdifferent model designs.\n883\t\n884\t8. Acknowledgments\n885\tThis work was supported in part by computational resources\n886\tprovided by the Center for Language and Speech Processing\n887\t(CLSP) at Johns Hopkins University. We thank Jan Vainer from\n888\tMeaning for his helpful assistance.\n889\t\n890\t9. Generative AI Use Disclosure\n891\tThe author used generative AI tools to assist with language polishing, grammar correction, writing organization, and LaTeX\n892\tformatting. All research ideas, experimental design, implementation, data analysis, results, and conclusions were developed\n893\tand finalized by the author. The author takes full responsibility\n894\tfor the content of this work.\n895\t\n896\t10. References\n897\t[1] K. C. Yuen, L. Haoyang, and C. E. Siong, “Asr model adaptation\n898\tfor rare words using synthetic data generated by multiple textto-speech systems,” in 2023 Asia Pacific Signal and Information\n899\tProcessing Association Annual Summit and Conference (APSIPA\n900\tASC). IEEE, 2023, pp. 1771–1778.\n901\t[2] H. Wu, J. Kang, L. Meng, H. Meng, and H.-y. Lee, “The\n902\tdefender’s perspective on automatic speaker verification: An\n903\toverview,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2305.12804, 2023.\n904\t[3] A. Khan, K. M. Malik, J. Ryan, and M. Saravanan, “Voice spoofing countermeasures: Taxonomy, state-of-the-art, experimental\n905\tanalysis of generalizability, open challenges, and the way forward,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.00417, 2022.\n906\t\n907\t\f[4] L. Pham, P. Lam, D. Tran, H. Tang, T. Nguyen, A. Schindler,\n908\tF. Skopik, A. Polonsky, and H. C. Vu, “A comprehensive survey\n909\twith critical analysis for deepfake speech detection,” Computer\n910\tScience Review, vol. 57, p. 100757, 2025.\n911\t[5] J. Zhao and W.-Q. Zhang, “Improving automatic speech recognition performance for low-resource languages with self-supervised\n912\tmodels,” IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Signal Processing,\n913\tvol. 16, no. 6, pp. 1227–1241, 2022.\n914\t[6] J.-w. Jung, W. Zhang, J. Shi, Z. Aldeneh, T. Higuchi, B.J. Theobald, A. H. Abdelaziz, and S. Watanabe, “Espnetspk: full pipeline speaker embedding toolkit with reproducible\n915\trecipes, self-supervised front-ends, and off-the-shelf models,”\n916\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2401.17230, 2024.\n917\t[7] M. Li, Y. Ahmadiadli, and X.-P. Zhang, “A survey on speech deepfake detection,” ACM Computing Surveys, vol. 57, no. 7, pp. 1–38,\n918\t2025.\n919\t[8] X. Chen, H. Wu, J.-S. R. Jang, and H.-y. Lee, “Singing\n920\tvoice graph modeling for singfake detection,” arXiv preprint\n921\tarXiv:2406.03111, 2024.\n922\t[9] Y. Zhu, S. Koppisetti, T. Tran, and G. Bharaj, “Slim: Stylelinguistics mismatch model for generalized audio deepfake detection,” Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems,\n923\tvol. 37, pp. 67 901–67 928, 2024.\n924\t[10] H. Tak, M. Todisco, X. Wang, J.-w. Jung, J. Yamagishi, and\n925\tN. Evans, “Automatic speaker verification spoofing and deepfake detection using wav2vec 2.0 and data augmentation,” arXiv\n926\tpreprint arXiv:2202.12233, 2022.\n927\t[11] Y. Yang, H. Qin, H. Zhou, C. Wang, T. Guo, K. Han, and Y. Wang,\n928\t“A robust audio deepfake detection system via multi-view feature,” in ICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International Conference on\n929\tAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2024,\n930\tpp. 13 131–13 135.\n931\t[12] L. Li, T. Lu, X. Ma, M. Yuan, and D. Wan, “Voice deepfake detection using the self-supervised pre-training model hubert,” Applied\n932\tsciences, vol. 13, no. 14, p. 8488, 2023.\n933\t[13] X. Chen, Y. Zhang, G. Zhu, and Z. Duan, “Ur channel-robust synthetic speech detection system for asvspoof 2021,” arXiv preprint\n934\tarXiv:2107.12018, 2021.\n935\t[14] Z. Ba, Q. Wen, P. Cheng, Y. Wang, F. Lin, L. Lu, and Z. Liu,\n936\t“Transferring audio deepfake detection capability across languages,” in Proceedings of the ACM Web Conference 2023, 2023,\n937\tpp. 2033–2044.\n938\t[15] Y. Zhang, G. Zhu, F. Jiang, and Z. Duan, “An empirical study\n939\ton channel effects for synthetic voice spoofing countermeasure\n940\tsystems,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2104.01320, 2021.\n941\t\n942\t[21] S. Chen, C. Wang, Z. Chen, Y. Wu, S. Liu, Z. Chen, J. Li,\n943\tN. Kanda, T. Yoshioka, X. Xiao et al., “Wavlm: Large-scale selfsupervised pre-training for full stack speech processing,” IEEE\n944\tJournal of Selected Topics in Signal Processing, vol. 16, no. 6,\n945\tpp. 1505–1518, 2022.\n946\t[22] W.-N. Hsu, B. Bolte, Y.-H. H. Tsai, K. Lakhotia, R. Salakhutdinov, and A. Mohamed, “Hubert: Self-supervised speech representation learning by masked prediction of hidden units,” IEEE/ACM\n947\ttransactions on audio, speech, and language processing, vol. 29,\n948\tpp. 3451–3460, 2021.\n949\t[23] A. Baevski, Y. Zhou, A. Mohamed, and M. Auli, “wav2vec\n950\t2.0: A framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations,” Advances in neural information processing systems,\n951\tvol. 33, pp. 12 449–12 460, 2020.\n952\t[24] A. Babu, C. Wang, A. Tjandra, K. Lakhotia, Q. Xu, N. Goyal,\n953\tK. Singh, P. Von Platen, Y. Saraf, J. Pino et al., “Xls-r: Selfsupervised cross-lingual speech representation learning at scale,”\n954\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2111.09296, 2021.\n955\t[25] A. Kulkarni, S. Dowerah, A. Kulkarni, T. Alumäe, and M. M.\n956\tDoss, “Do compact ssl backbones matter for audio deepfake\n957\tdetection? a controll